kernel/generic_raw_uart.c: the instance outlives an open file whose device is gone

A raw-uart that is in use when its USB device leaves the bus (an HmIP-RFUSB
or HB-RF-USB pulled, or deauthorized through sysfs) panics the kernel at the
next close: hb_rf_usb_2_disconnect() has dropped its reference, so the last
generic_raw_uart_close() drops the port's last one from inside
stop_connection() - hb_rf_usb_2_delete() calls generic_raw_uart_remove(),
which frees the instance - and then runs up(&instance->sem) and
instance->driver->owner on freed memory:

  Unable to handle kernel read from unreadable memory at virtual address 0
  pc : __list_del_entry_valid_or_report+0x14/0x120
  lr : __up.isra.0+0x28/0x90
  Call trace:
   up+0xec/0xf8
   generic_raw_uart_close+0x74/0xd0 [generic_raw_uart]
   __fput+0xe0/0x320

The embedded struct cdev dies with the instance as well, while __fput()
still has to cdev_put() it after release.

The class device is now embedded in the instance and owns its lifetime: it
is registered with cdev_device_add(), so the cdev holds a reference to it and
every open file holds the cdev; generic_raw_uart_remove() only drops the
probe's reference. The instance is freed by the device's release callback
when the driver has removed it and the last file has been closed - in
whichever order. The close takes the module owner before stop_connection().
The probe's error path frees through the same release and gives the device
number back (it used to unregister a part of the module's chrdev region).

Seen on openccu-lite (Raspberry Pi 4, kernel 6.18, init_on_free=1,
panic_on_oops=1) with an HmIP-RFUSB held open by multimacd: the deauthorize,
then `systemctl stop multimacd` rebooted the system every time.

Upstream: Not submitted yet (alexreinert/piVCCU)

Signed-off-by: Sebastian Raff <hobbyquaker@gmail.com>

